BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Out of Sight
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260819T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260819T223000
DTSTAMP:20260816T082840Z
UID:019dd532-0b98-b1eb-af29-bc9f09d740b6@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: film editors Stephen Rivkin\, Robb Sullivan\, John 
 Axelrad\, and Emma E. Hickox \nSelected by the Film Editors Branch\n\nWedn
 esday\, August 19\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion 
 Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 3m\n\n1998 | 123 min. | USA | Color | English | Ra
 ted R | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Steven Soderbergh \nWRITTEN BY: Scott Frank \nWI
 TH: George Clooney\, Jennifer Lopez\, Ving Rhames\, Don Cheadle\n\nType: S
 creenings\n\nBank robber Jake Foley’s prison escape is complicated by an
  inconvenient encounter with U.S. marshal Karen Sisco\, and an unexpected 
 attraction between them. Making his first major studio film\, director Ste
 ven Soderbergh\, working with an Oscar nominated screenplay by Scott Frank
 \, brings the world of master crime novelist Elmore Leonard to the screen 
 in a dazzling combination of old-fashioned Hollywood glamour and contempor
 ary grit. The film was also nominated for its editing\, by the accomplishe
 d Anne V. Coates (Lawrence of Arabia\, 1962)\, who employs intercutting\, 
 flashbacks\, and freeze frames to enhance the swooningly romantic chemistr
 y of stars George Clooney and Jennifer Lopez.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/out-of-sight-019dd532
 -0b98-b1eb-af29-bc9f09d740b6
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Marilyn & Me with Lawrence Schiller Book Signing and Conversation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260820T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260820T190000
DTSTAMP:20260816T082840Z
UID:019faf26-8fef-003d-559e-344bbcac0055@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Book Signings
DESCRIPTION:Bestselling author and filmmaker Lawrence Schiller shares his 
 memories of photographing Hollywood icon Marilyn Monroe\, and signs copies
  of his new book\, in an exclusive appearance with museum curator Sophia S
 errano.\n\nThursday\, August 20\, 2026 at 6 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nType: Book Signings\n\nIn a pre-signing conversatio
 n about Marilyn & Me\, the author shares his personal account of photograp
 hing Monroe during the production of her unfinished final film\, Something
 's Got to Give (1962). Schiller reveals insights into his remarkable caree
 r\; the stories behind his iconic photographs\; and his thoughts on the le
 gacy of one of Hollywood's most celebrated stars.\nAfterward\, he signs co
 pies his new work\, richly illustrated with more than 100 photographs—in
 cluding rare images from the set of Monroe's final film. The book offers a
 n intimate portrait of the actress in the final months of her life\, along
 side Schiller's recollections of the moments they shared.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/marilyn-me-with-lawre
 nce-schiller-book-signing-conversation-019faf26-8fef-003d-559e-344bbcac005
 5
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Searchers in 70mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260820T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260820T223000
DTSTAMP:20260816T082840Z
UID:019de50c-7daf-6b53-eb10-eaee76152d11@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, August 20\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 59m\n\n1956 | 119 min. | USA | Colo
 r | English | 70mm \nDIRECTED BY: John Ford \nWRITTEN BY: Frank S. Nugent
  \nWITH: John Wayne\, Jeffrey Hunter\, Vera Miles\, Ward Bond\n\nType: Scr
 eenings\n\nWhen the family of Civil War veteran Ethan Edwards (John Wayne)
  is killed in a Comanche raid\, he spends years looking for his kidnapped 
 niece—but her uncle may prove more of a threat than her abductors. John 
 Ford’s epic\, morally complex Western drama has become one of his most r
 evered films in the 70 years since its release. It has been cited as a fav
 orite by many filmmakers\, including Francis Ford Coppola\, Martin Scorses
 e and Steven Spielberg\, and has influenced films as diverse as Star Wars 
 (1977) and Hardcore (1979). Three-time Oscar winner Winton C. Hoch (The Qu
 iet Man\, 1952) shot the film in VistaVision on stunning outdoor locations
 \, including Monument Valley\, culminating in a powerful\, frequently imit
 ated final image.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-searchers-019de50
 c-7daf-6b53-eb10-eaee76152d11
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Gallery Spotlight: Cinematic Makeup and Hairstyling with Ve Neill
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260821T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260821T130000
DTSTAMP:20260816T082840Z
UID:019dc075-f108-7589-f25f-e0d3f3711c8f@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, August 21\, 2026 at 12 pm\n\nLocation: Sidney Poitier
  Grand Lobby\n\nRuntime: 1h\n\nCost: Free with general admission\n\nType: 
 Conversations\n\nThree-time Academy Award winner Ve Neill (Ed Wood\, 1994 
 and Mrs. Doubtfire\, 1993) explores the secrets behind Hollywood’s most 
 memorable character looks. She also highlights the design of dark and maca
 bre characters from Sweeney Todd: The Demon Barber of Fleet Street (2007)\
 , currently on display in the Identity gallery. \nAcademy Award winner How
 ard Berger moderates.
LOCATION:Sidney Poitier Grand Lobby
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/gallery-spotlight-cin
 ematic-makeup-and-hairstyling-with-ve-neill-019dc075-f108-7589-f25f-e0d3f3
 711c8f
END:VEVENT
